# Line-ending and diff policy.
#
# The default is deliberately hands-off: markdown, JSON and .uiproj files are checked out in
# the platform's own line endings, which is why a fetched template diffs against the source on
# every text file while being byte-identical in content. That costs a noisy diff and nothing
# else -- see the project-scaffolding skill, references/fetched-files-crlf.md.
#
# Shell scripts are the exception, and not a cosmetic one. With core.autocrlf=true (the Git for
# Windows default) a committed `#!/usr/bin/env bash\n` is checked out as `...bash\r\n`, and
# `bash\r` is not the name of any interpreter. The script then fails to execute on Linux, macOS,
# WSL and Git Bash with a message that names the whole shebang and explains nothing. Both
# scripts in this repo ship with a shebang, so both need eol=lf.

* text=auto

# Executed by an interpreter named in the shebang -- must be LF everywhere.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Interpreted by PowerShell, which accepts either, but keep them consistent across platforms.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Test fixture config (tests/fixtures/**/Config*.tsv). TAB-separated, and the tab is the field
# separator -- a column that becomes spaces makes the fixture silently build an empty workbook,
# which reads as "the rule stopped firing". Pinned to LF so the file is byte-identical on every
# platform; the tabs themselves are not line endings and are not converted, but pinning removes
# one way for an editor to be helpful about it.
*.tsv text eol=lf

# Binary: never line-ending-converted, never diffed as text. An .xlsx is a zip; converting it
# corrupts it.
*.xlsx binary
*.xlsm binary
*.nupkg binary
*.pdf binary
*.pptx binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
